# Agent 2 Architecture Rules

Follow the canonical workflow in `docs/01_agent2_architecture.md` §5 for
any pipeline-shaped implementation work.

## Whole network first

The complete `FullNetwork` must be assembled from the Agent 1 handoff
before any kinetic-law assignment, parameter declaration, or boundary
assessment occurs. Never skip part of the curated network because it does
not obviously belong to a module.

## Kinetic-law assignment before boundary assessment

Every reaction's kinetic-law structure must already be assigned before
boundary assessment begins — a boundary heuristic may use kinetic-law
structure as a signal.

## Parameter declaration/initialization before boundary assessment

Every kinetic law's parameters must be declared (with a `ParameterSource`
and an initial value) before boundary assessment begins — a boundary
heuristic may use parameter magnitudes as a signal.

## Boundary likelihood is qualitative

A module boundary's likelihood is always one of `VERY_LOW`/`LOW`/
`MEDIUM`/`HIGH`/`VERY_HIGH` (`BoundaryLikelihood`). Never compute or
display a numeric probability for it.

## Module decomposition is derived from the full model

A `ModuleDecomposition`/`ModuleSpecification` only ever references
elements that already exist in the `FullNetwork`/`ModelSpecification`. It
is never built from a separately re-derived or partial network.

## The full model is authoritative

The full `ModelSpecification` and full Antimony model are the canonical
representation. Every `ModuleSpecification`/`ModuleAntimonyArtifact` must
remain traceable back to it (via `source_boundary_ids` and the shared
`ModelSpecification`/`FullNetwork` ids it references).

## Standalone modules require explicit boundary interfaces

A `ModuleAntimonyArtifact`'s standalone variant may only be produced for a
module whose `boundary_interfaces` are fully explicit
(`ModuleBoundaryInterface` for every externally-connected species). Never
invent a boundary condition to make a module "simulatable" — omit the
standalone artifact for that module instead.
